  "query_preview": "..\nLook for shower potential to increase during the next 12 to 24 hours,\nas a subtle surface trough passes overhead. Originally, the models\nportrayed this as the leading edge of stronger 15 to 20 knot trade\nwinds, but, this morning's scatterometer pass showed these winds have\nalready expanded west-southwest across the region ahead of the\ntrough. This is due to the pressure gradient tightening a little\nfaster than the models show, which was anticipated, as high pressure\ncontinues to strengthen to the north between Iwo To and \nMinamitorishima. It wouldn't be surprising if we end up seeing \nsustained winds increase from 15 to 20 knots currently analyzed in \nthe surge, into the 20 to 25 knot range by week's end. \n\nAlthough the trough is passing overhead tonight, moisture is \nsomewhat anemic for this time of year. As such, look for mainly \nisolated showers to develop tonight. Additional troughs upstream \ntowards Wake Island will lead to periods of scattered showers \ndeveloping as we head towards the weekend. Depending on trough \namplitude, if enough moisture flux and surface convergence develop, \nisolated thunderstorms couldn't be ruled out, although omega values \nare rather paltry in the time height soundings. Still, it's something\nwe'll keep an eye on.",
